# References

## What is a Reference?

A reference is an alias (another name) for an existing variable.

---

## Reference Declaration

**Syntax:**

```cpp
data_type &reference_name = variable_name;
```

Example:

```cpp
int num = 10;
int &ref = num;
```

---

## How References Work

Both the variable and its reference point to the same memory location.

```cpp
cout << num;  // 10
cout << ref;  // 10
```

---

## Modifying Through a Reference

Changing the reference also changes the original variable.

```cpp
ref = 20;

cout << num;  // 20
```

---

## References vs Pointers

| References | Pointers |
|------------|----------|
| Alias of a variable | Stores a memory address |
| Must be initialized | Can be initialized later |
| Cannot be null | Can be `nullptr` |
| Cannot be changed to refer to another variable | Can point to different variables |

---

## Advantages of References

- Simpler syntax than pointers.
- Avoids unnecessary copying of data.
- Commonly used for function parameters.

---

## Important Points

- A reference is another name for a variable.
- References must be initialized when declared.
- A reference cannot refer to another variable after initialization.
- Changes made through a reference affect the original variable.
